<p>The <a href="https://www.apaonline.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">American Philosophical Association</a> (APA) has announced the winners of its 2026 Dr. Martin R. Lebowitz and Eve Lewellis Lebowitz Prize.<span id="more-58169"></span></p>
<p>They are:</p>
<p style="padding-left: 40px;"><a href="https://scholars.duke.edu/person/felipe.debrigard" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>Felipe De Brigard</strong></a>, Professor of Philosophy and Psychology &amp; Neuroscience, a member of the Center for Cognitive Neuroscience at Duke University, and Principal Investigator of the <a href="https://www.imclab.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Imagination and Modal Cognition Laboratory</a> (IMC Lab) at the Duke Institute for Brain Science</p>
<p>and</p>
<p style="padding-left: 40px;"><a href="https://www.sarahkrobins.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>Sarah Robins</strong></a>, Professor of Philosophy at Purdue University and founding co-director of Purdue’s <a href="https://www.cla.purdue.edu/academic/philosophy/research/cognition-agency-and-intelligence-center/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cognition, Agency, and Intelligence Center</a> (CAIC).</p>
<div id="attachment_58171" style="width: 893px" class="wp-caption aligncenter"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" aria-describedby="caption-attachment-58171" class="size-full wp-image-58171" src="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/de-brigard-and-robins.png" alt="" width="883" height="449" srcset="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/de-brigard-and-robins.png 883w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/de-brigard-and-robins-300x153.png 300w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/de-brigard-and-robins-768x391.png 768w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/de-brigard-and-robins-400x203.png 400w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 883px) 100vw, 883px" /><p id="caption-attachment-58171" class="wp-caption-text">Felipe de Brigard and Sarah Robins</p></div>
<p>The Lebowitz Prize is jointly awarded by the APA and the <a href="https://www.pbk.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Phi Beta Kappa Society</a>. Its winners must be two philosophers who hold contrasting views on a chosen topic of current interest in philosophy. They present their views and engage in a dialogue at an annual Lebowitz symposium, held during an APA divisional meeting. Each winner receives an honorarium of $25,000.</p>
<p>You can learn more about the prize and see a list of its past winners <a href="https://www.apaonline.org/page/lebowitz" target="_blank" rel="noopener">here</a>.</p><p>The post <a href="https://dailynous.com/2026/08/26/de-brigard-and-robins-win-2026-lebowitz-prize/">De Brigard and Robins Win 2026 Lebowitz Prize</a> first appeared on <a href="https://dailynous.com">Daily Nous</a>.</p>